  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal agencies obligated $70.9B in contracts in Texas in FY2026 to date. That total comes from 152,342 awards with an average award size of $466K, and obligations fell 17.8% year over year. The Department of Defense led buying in the state with $29.3B, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $22B and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $14.8B. Construction and health-related industries account for large shares: Commercial and Institutional Building Construction received $17.5B, Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing received $9.8B, and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing received $9.6B. A small number of vendors captured substantial dollars: LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION appears twice in the vendor list with $10.1B and $6.4B, and MCKESSON CORPORATION received $9.7B. Year-by-year obligations show volatility, from $55.1B in FY21 up to $86.3B in FY25 and then to the FY2026 to date total of $70.9B. Readers should note current-year totals are partial. This snapshot shows where federal buying concentrated by agency, vendor, and industry in the state so far this fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What are the main drivers of federal obligations in Texas this fiscal year?",
        "body": "Defense, homeland security, and veterans affairs are the largest buyers, accounting for $29.3B, $22B, and $14.8B respectively. Industry concentration is strong in construction and health and aerospace manufacturing, with Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $17.5B, Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing at $9.8B, and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $9.6B. Large prime vendors also shape totals: LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION appears with $10.1B and $6.4B and MCKESSON CORPORATION shows $9.7B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are awards by count and size?",
        "body": "There were 152,342 awards with an average award size of $466K, showing many transactions and a midrange average. Total obligations for FY2026 to date are $70.9B, down 17.8% year over year, so dollar concentration shifted compared with prior fiscal years. Top vendors and top industries capture large shares of that $70.9B, indicating concentration in a limited set of firms and NAICS sectors."
      }
    ]
  },
